@startuml activities_modelview_contrast
' ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
' activities_modelview_contrast — Upcoming (v20/v20.1) vs Activities (v20.2).
' Converted from Mermaid in the v26.8 audit (indexed, never rendered).
'
' Why two pipelines instead of one reusable one: they answer different
' questions. Upcoming asks a FIXED question ("what is coming up?") and can
' afford an expensive granular diff because the answer changes underneath a
' user who is staring at it. Activities asks a PARAMETERISED question ("what
' is in this category?") and changes the whole question on every rail click
' — diffing across two different questions is meaningless, so it resets.
'
' Same base class (TaskSnapshotModel, v20.3), opposite refresh strategies.
' That contrast IS the lesson; collapsing them into one model would have
' hidden it.
' ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
!include _style.puml

title Two model/view pipelines, deliberately unalike

package "Upcoming page (v20 / v20.1)" {
  component "TaskListModel\nFIXED query: upcomingTasks()\ngranular diff on change" as um
  component "TaskFilterProxy\npriority lens + due-date sort" as up
  component "QListView\n+ TaskCardDelegate (cards)" as uv
  um --> up
  up --> uv
}

package "Activities detail pane (v20.2)" {
  component "CategoryTaskModel\nPARAMETERISED: tasksIn(categoryId)\nre-pointable · plain reset" as am
  component "QListView\n+ CategoryTaskDelegate (flat rows)" as av
  am --> av
}

component "rail click" as rail
rail --> am : setCategoryId(id)

note bottom of am
  <b>No proxy here, on purpose.</b>
  No lens to apply, and insertion order
  is the order the user typed things in —
  sorting it would destroy information
  the user created.
end note

note bottom of um
  A proxy exists here because the SAME set
  of tasks must be viewable through several
  lenses without the model knowing about any
  of them. Sorting/filtering is the proxy's
  job; supplying rows is the model's.
end note
@enduml
